' ============================================================================
' 45 The semi-naive iteration of a Boolean monotone component (class R2).
'
' Source of Figure TL11 in ../tensor-logic/dynamic-semantics.md (section 5.5).
' The figure restates SEMI-NAIVE of that section (Bancilhon and Ramakrishnan
' 1986). It adds no new fact.
'
' Reading order:
'   1. R and the delta start with the facts and the non-recursive rules.
'   2. Each round evaluates the rules with at least one atom from the delta,
'      in canonical order, and reserves the work of each join first.
'   3. The new delta is the derived tuples that are not in R, and R grows.
'   4. An empty delta ends the loop: R is the least fixpoint.
'
' Colors come from concept-colors.iuml (aliases of the shared palette).
' Render: ./render.sh 45-tl-semi-naive.puml   Check: ./render.sh --check

@startuml 45-tl-semi-naive
!include ../../../casper/theory/diagrams/palette.iuml
!include concept-colors.iuml
$ca_base_skin()
skinparam ArrowColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor $ca_note_amber
skinparam PartitionBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam NoteBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBackgroundColor $ca_note_grey
title The semi-naive iteration: SEMI-NAIVE(C)

start
:a component C of class R2, in one stratum; <<$rrs_tl_term>>
:<latex>R \leftarrow \Delta \leftarrow</latex> the facts and the
non-recursive rules of C; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
while (<latex>\Delta \neq \emptyset</latex>?) is (yes)
  :reserve the work of each join
  before it runs; <<$rrs_meter>>
  :<latex>N \leftarrow</latex> the rules of C, evaluated with
  at least one atom from <latex>\Delta</latex>, in canonical order; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
  :<latex>\Delta \leftarrow N \setminus R</latex>
  <latex>R \leftarrow R \cup \Delta</latex>; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
endwhile (no)
:R is the least fixpoint of C
(Knaster and Tarski); <<$rrs_effect>>
note right
  A round never derives a fact from old facts
  only, so the number of rounds is at most the
  number of possible derived tuples.
end note
stop

legend bottom
**Concept colors**
|<$rrs_tl_term>   | the component |
|<$rrs_tl_engine> | a step of the iteration |
|<$rrs_meter>     | a reservation on the host-work meter |
|<$rrs_effect>    | the result |
endlegend
@enduml
